Subject: [PATCH 14/15] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 5, patch 14;
 row G of its card): THE HISTORY OF AUTHORSHIP IS READ IN ALL FOUR FIELDS

Review 79, the second reader's row 25, its second part: the gate `attribution` read one field of four in the
history, the author's name. Made again on the commit before this patch, in small repositories: a commit whose
committer is someone else, a commit authored under the repository's name and another address, and a vendor
token in a name or an address, each passed. They stand in the patch's test, with the reading that stood before,
which reports none of them.

The rule is main's (its M11): not a list of names. After the commit the rule is read from, the history holds
one identity as author and as committer: the name `originaxiom`, and the address under which that commit was
authored. No address is written into the gate: it is read from the commit. The vendor tokens the text gate
holds are looked for in all four fields. The trailer rule is as it was.

One line is given for a commit's identity, naming the first field that differs, so that main's tests of a
foreign author read as they did. An address is not printed. What git gives is decoded without a crash: a name
that is not UTF-8 is read with its bad bytes replaced, and is then not the repository's name.

On main nothing moves: every commit after da25c4f49 (784 at bf76e5ab1) carries one identity in all four
fields, counted by the test on the repository itself. No test of main's is changed; its eight pass as they
were.

What the card said could go wrong, and stands: a commit that another hand made honestly fails by name, a
merge made on the web for one, or a patch applied with its author's name kept. Such a commit is main's to
judge.

Not checked by main. Tested against planted errors by the session that wrote it: eight planted commits and a
twin.
